tal line at exactly one point, each diagonal line at exactly one point. So a sunny line can cover at most one point per column, per row, per diagonal. Since there are n columns (a=1..n) and n rows (b=1..n) but not all rows are fully present because of triangular shape. But still, each sunny line can cover at most one point in each column.\n\nThus with two sunny lines, we can cover at most two points per column. Since there are n columns, we can cover at most 2n points with sunny lines. The remaining points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover all points in a column), horizontal (cover all points in a row), or diagonal (cover all points on a diagonal). So we need to cover the rest.\n\nBut perhaps we can use vertical lines for many columns, leaving at most two columns uncovered (since we have only two sunny lines). Actually we have n-2 non-sunny lines, which could be vertical lines covering n-2 columns completely. Then the remaining two columns must be covered by sunny lines. However, each column has multiple points (n+1-a points). For column n-1, there are 2 points; for column n, there is 1 point. So we need to cover these points with two sunny lines. As we saw, it's impossible because any line covering two points in these columns is non-sunny. So that arrangement fails.\n\nBut maybe we can use some non-sunny lines that are not vertical, e.g., horizontal lines covering rows, which might reduce the number of points left in the last columns. Let's explore.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines. Suppose we use some horizontal lines to cover rows, which may reduce the number of points left in the rightmost columns. For example, we could use a horizontal line y=1 covering all points with b=1 across all columns (i.e., points (a,1) for a=1..n). That covers one point in each column. Then we could use vertical lines for n-3 other columns, covering all points in those columns. Then we have left points in the remaining column(s) that are not cover